  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nCentral Illinois remains positioned along the edge of a strong \nupper-level ridge with a Ring of Fire pattern resulting in daily\nconvective chances in the region, mainly to our north. \nThe advertised backdoor cold front will sag across the CWA \ntoday and reach southeast Illinois around max heating. The \nairmass will be quite unstable, with MLCAPE over 4000 J/kg; \nhowever, shear will be very weak at only around 15kts. Still, \nscattered showers and thunderstorms are still possible in \nsoutheast IL this afternoon. Since shear is weak along this\nfront, will keep pops in the 20-30% range, which matches well \nwith the marginal risk issued by SPC. These scattered storms \nshould push east and south quickly or dissipate quickly, leaving\ntonight dry.\n\nSince the backdoor front is slowly sagging south today, the high\nheat and humidity is still expected to continue into the\nafternoon. Temperatures in the 90s to around 100, along with\ntriple-digit heat index values are still forecast across the\nentire CWA. Therefore, no changes are planned for the Extreme\nHeat Warning, which will still end at 7 pm tonight. \n\nThe front will push the high pressure ridge back to the west,\nwhich will being relief to the heat and humidity across the\nregion for Sunday and Monday, though this could be temporary.\nSurface winds will become northeast and this will bring drier\ndewpoints (in the 60s) into the area. Combined with \"cooler\" \ntemperatures in the mid to upper 80s, we will finally get a \nbreak from the triple-digit heat index values...though it will \nstill feel like summer. \n\nThe upper level ridge will become flattened and elongated from\nwest to east as a strong trough develops in the northwest and\nmoves east across the northern US, giving us a zonal pattern \nfor the rest of the week. Though temperatures will once again\nwarm back into the 90s for Tue and Wed, this will only be for a\ncouple of days and the dewpoints will NOT return the the 70s for\nthe rest of the week. Heat index values for Tue will remain\nbelow 100; however, values just over 100 are expected Wed\nafternoon south of I-72/Danville. \n\nDry weather is expected Tue into Wed, but the the fast zonal\nflow will allow a cold front to move south across the area, \nbringing a chance of showers and storms Wed night into Thursday.\nThis will also bring cooler temperatures to the area for Thur \nand Friday. Due to the zonal flow, the front will not drop out\nof the CWA; therefore, small chances of precip over parts of the\nCWA will have to be maintained for Fri and Sat.",
